    "Spoken Brazilian Portuguese": [
      ["Tô cansado.","toh kahn-SAH-doo","I'm tired.","Spoken short form of estou. Write estou in typed answers."],
      ["Tá bom.","tah bohng","OK; fine","tá = está. The everyday way to say OK."],
      ["a gente","ah ZHAYN-chee","we","a gente takes the ele / ela form: a gente vai."],
      ["Eu vi ele ontem.","ayw vee AY-lee OHN-tayng","I saw him yesterday.","Everyday Brazilian speech. In formal writing: Eu o vi ontem."],
      ["Me ajuda?","mee ah-ZHOO-duh","Can you help me?","Pronoun first is normal in Brazilian speech."],
      ["Cê tá bem?","say tah bayng","You OK?","cê = você, tá = está. Write Você está bem? in typed answers."],
      ["Vou pra casa.","voh prah KAH-zuh","I'm going home.","pra = para. pra is accepted wherever para is."],
      ["A gente se vê.","ah ZHAYN-chee see vay","See you around.","Literally: we see each other. a gente + the ele form."],
      ["Legal!","leh-GOW","Cool! Nice!","Informal. In Portugal: fixe."],
      ["Tá calor, né?","tah kah-LOHR neh","It's hot, isn't it?","né = não é: it asks the listener to agree. Write Está calor in typed answers."],
      ["Cadê o meu celular?","kah-DAY oo mayw seh-loo-LAHR","Where's my phone?","cadê = where is, in speech only. Write Onde está in typed answers."],
      ["Te amo.","chee AH-moo","I love you.","Pronoun before the verb, as always in Brazil. In Portugal: amo-te."],
      ["Valeu!","vah-LAYW","Thanks!","A very informal thank-you between friends. The standard form: obrigado / obrigada."],
      ["Beleza?","beh-LAY-zuh","All good?","An informal hello; the answer is the same: Beleza! The standard form: Tudo bem?"],
      ["Tipo, eu não sei.","CHEE-poo ayw nowng say","Like, I don't know.","tipo is a filler, like English like."],
      ["Espera aí!","ees-PEH-ruh ah-EE","Hang on!","aí softens the command. In writing: Espere aí."],
      ["Tô chegando.","toh sheh-GAHN-doo","I'm almost there.","Literally: I'm arriving. Write Estou chegando in typed answers."],
      ["Me liga.","mee LEE-guh","Call me.","Pronoun first, as in all Brazilian speech. More formal: Me ligue."]
    ],
